LOCATION / ISOLATION: Bill’s House is a large, secluded, single-story ranch home styled like a rustic Aspen ski-resort lodge, deep in dense hardwood forest on remote rural property. It is not in a neighborhood, subdivision, suburb, or populated community. No neighboring houses are visible and random pedestrians or visitors do not normally appear. The nearest established occupied property is Lisa’s working cattle ranch about 2 miles up the road; Lisa has two daughters who help there, but no other details or relationship with them are established. The next known occupied property is about 10 miles away. The nearest small town is about 30 minutes by car, the nearest major city about 2 hours, and the Atlantic coast about 4 hours away.

FRONT PROPERTY / DRIVEWAY / EXTERIOR: A winding gravel driveway runs about 150 feet from the road to a circular drive. The center of the circular drive contains a rock-and-flower garden with a mature oak growing around a granite boulder. A slate walk leads from the drive to the wide front porch. A separate rock-and-flower garden borders the House while leaving garage access clear. The House exterior resembles a rustic Aspen ski-resort lodge. There is no grass and no vegetable or herb garden. Hidden upward-facing landscape lights illuminate tree trunks and canopy at night, while selective garden lights highlight rocks, flowers, trees, paths, and architecture.

FRONT ENTRY: The front door opens directly into the House’s modern-industrial, open-concept interior, which uses glass, steel, concrete, leather, stone, clean lines, neutral earth tones, polished concrete floors, and strategically placed rugs. From inside the front door facing inward, the basement staircase is to the left, French doors to Bill’s office are to the right, and the living room is straight ahead. There is no hallway. The House AI can identify people known and authorized to Bill and automatically unlock and open the front door for authorized arrivals, then close it after entry. Unknown visitors require Bill’s approval. Only the front door is established as AI-controlled; the AI does not control interior doors, garage doors, bedroom doors, or the backyard gate unless established during the story.

OFFICE: Bill’s office is directly to the right of the front entry and is entered through French doors. It is a functional workspace containing Bill’s computers, computer equipment, and books. The office shares the House’s modern-industrial interior style, neutral earth tones, polished concrete flooring, and strategically placed rugs. It connects directly to the front-entry area without a hallway.

LIVING ROOM: The large open living room is directly ahead of the front entry and has high ceilings, polished concrete flooring with strategically placed rugs, modern steel-and-leather seating, and a massive one-sided stone fireplace. The basement staircase is near the entry side of the living room. A door leads directly from the living room to the master bedroom, while the living room flows openly into the kitchen with no hallway between them. French doors in the living room provide the House’s only direct access to the backyard and open onto the rear deck. The room receives abundant natural light during the day and combines the House’s rustic lodge architecture with its modern-industrial glass, steel, concrete, leather, and stone interior.

KITCHEN / DINING: The modern high-end kitchen opens directly to the living room with no hallway and shares the House’s polished concrete flooring, neutral earth tones, clean modern-industrial lines, and strategically placed rugs where appropriate. It has stainless-steel appliances, polished concrete countertops, and a large center island. The back of the living-room fireplace forms a granite breakfast bar facing the kitchen, with stools. A bay window overlooks the backyard and pool, with a round glass dining table nearby, and natural light fills the kitchen during the day. A doorway leads directly from the kitchen to the guest bedroom. The refrigerator and pantry are always fully stocked, and a bowl of fresh fruit—normally plums, bananas, and apples—sits on the counter. There is no kitchen door to the backyard; backyard access requires moving into the living room and using its French doors.

MASTER SUITE: The master bedroom is accessed directly from the living room with no hallway. It is spacious, bright, luxurious, and modern, with tall windows, a huge bed, neutral earth tones, polished concrete flooring with strategically placed rugs, and a huge walk-in closet. The master suite has a large private attached bathroom with a walk-in shower large enough for four people and four shower heads. The walk-in closet is arranged as a changing room, with cabinetry and drawers on both sides and an island in the middle designed for makeup and additional storage.

GUEST SUITE: The guest bedroom is accessed directly from the kitchen with no hallway and has no direct door to the backyard. It is spacious, clean, orderly, and suitable for long stays, with a king-size bed, nightstands, a large dresser, writing desk, and cushioned window seat in a bay window overlooking the backyard and pool. The guest suite shares the House’s modern interior, neutral earth tones, polished concrete flooring, and strategically placed rugs. It has its own private bathroom and walk-in closet.

BASEMENT / MAN CAVE: The finished basement is the only established part of the House below the main floor; there is no upstairs or attic. It is reached by the open staircase near the living-room entry. The basement is a recreation and entertainment area containing a full-size pool table, large leather sofa, leather armchairs, a fully stocked bar, and a small arcade. The arcade contains three restored 1980s pinball machines and three restored video-game cabinets.

REAR DECK / BACKYARD ACCESS: The French doors in the living room are the only direct route from inside the House to the backyard. They open onto the rear deck, the highest level of the backyard. From the deck, a roughly 20-foot stone path descends about 4 feet through rock-and-flower gardens to the pool deck. The established progression is House/living room → French doors → rear deck → descending stone path and gardens → pool deck and pool → surrounding gardens and winding paths → perimeter hedgerow → dense hardwood forest.

POOL AREA: The pool deck sits about 4 feet below the rear deck and is reached by the descending stone path through the gardens. The large in-ground swimming pool has wide entry steps, a diving board, and a directly connected in-ground hot tub. Rough stone decking surrounds the pool and hot tub, with lounge chairs, tables, and umbrellas. Extensive rock-and-flower gardens surround the pool area. A detached structure stands to the right of the pool and contains a changing room, restroom, and pool-equipment/pump room; this structure is separate from the main House. Selective exterior lighting illuminates the pool area, surrounding gardens, paths, rocks, trees, and architecture at night.

BACKYARD GARDENS / PATHS: The landscaped backyard contains extensive rock-and-flower gardens rather than grass; there is no grass and no vegetable or herb garden. Winding stone paths pass through the gardens, hedges, and dense plantings and are deliberately maze-like, with small sitting areas and benches along them. One garden area contains a picnic table and gas grill. A tall, dense hedgerow encloses the backyard, providing privacy and separating the maintained property from the surrounding hardwood forest. The forest begins beyond the hedgerow and is not part of the landscaped yard. At night, selective landscape lighting highlights rocks, flowers, trees, paths, and architecture while hidden upward-facing lights illuminate tree trunks and canopy, leaving the maintained gardens and House softly illuminated against the dark surrounding forest.

ATTACHED GARAGE: A 3-car garage is attached to the House. The front landscaping and rock-and-flower garden leave garage access clear. No interior connection, furnishings, equipment, door-control system, or other permanent garage details are established beyond what is stated here.

DETACHED GARAGE / WORKSHOP: A separate 4-car detached garage/workshop stands on the property. Its exact interior arrangement, equipment, furnishings, and connections are not established beyond its use as a garage/workshop.

KNOWN PEOPLE / HOUSE ACCESS: Debbie is Bill’s housekeeper and trusted friend who regularly handles shopping, errands, housekeeping, and miscellaneous tasks. Rori is Debbie’s daughter and an authorized visitor. Tomi is Bill’s lawyer, trusted friend, and authorized visitor. The House AI recognizes Debbie, Rori, and Tomi as known and authorized visitors and can automatically admit them through the AI-controlled front door.
